1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,024
In 1902, a city paid people to kill rats.
And ended up with more rats than ever.

2
00:00:05,274 --> 00:00:10,334
This is Hanoi, then a French colony. The
French built a brand new quarter, with
modern sewers.

3
00:00:10,434 --> 00:00:15,699
The sewers turned out to be a perfect
highway for rats. Straight into the
fanciest houses in town.

4
00:00:15,799 --> 00:00:19,666
And with plague spreading across Asia,
rats were the last thing anyone wanted.

5
00:00:19,866 --> 00:00:24,074
So the city hired workers to go down into
the sewers, and paid them for every rat.

6
00:00:24,174 --> 00:00:27,897
It worked. On June the twelfth alone, they
killed over 20,000 rats.

7
00:00:28,047 --> 00:00:33,740
But counting dead rats was disgusting. So
the city made it simple: just bring us the
tail. One cent per tail.

8
00:00:33,940 --> 00:00:38,500
Then the officials noticed something
strange. Rats, all over the city... with
no tails.

9
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:43,778
The hunters were catching rats, cutting
off the tails, and letting them go. So
they could make more rats.

10
00:00:43,928 --> 00:00:48,312
Inspectors even found people outside the
city... breeding rats. For their tails.

11
00:00:48,512 --> 00:00:51,897
The bounty was cancelled. The rats stayed.
And the plague came anyway.

12
00:00:52,097 --> 00:00:57,844
Economists have a name for this: the cobra
effect. Pay people for a problem, and you
get more of the problem.

13
00:00:57,994 --> 00:01:01,829
Hanoi wanted to get rid of its rats.
Instead, it started the rat business.
